# =============================================================================
# metro-be AWS smoke test: https://metro.test.name.vn (Task 8)
#
# Client: VS Code REST Client (humao.rest-client), which Kiro can install.
#   - Requests use `# @name` and `{{name.response.body.$.x}}` to chain values.
#   - Cookies are remembered per host (rest-client.rememberCookiesForSubsequentRequests,
#     on by default). Login sets the HttpOnly `token` cookie; the gateway turns it into
#     `Authorization: Bearer <jwt>` and adds X-API-KEY itself, so no API key or
#     Authorization header is sent from here.
#   - Run the requests top to bottom with "Send Request".
#
# Two users are needed because the endpoints check roles:
#   station / ticket-type create -> ROLE_ADMIN, order create -> ROLE_CUSTOMER.
#   New users are ROLE_CUSTOMER; the admin is promoted with a one-off ECS task
#   (step 1.4, PowerShell commands in the Task 8 notes).
#
# Register needs an OTP. With the placeholder SMTP credentials the e-mail send fails
# (send-otp may answer 500), but the OTP is already stored in Redis under
# OTP:<purpose>:<email>. Read it with ECS Exec (Task 8 notes) and paste it below.
#
# Bump @run before every new run (emails, usernames and codes must be unique).
# =============================================================================
@baseUrl = https://metro.test.name.vn
@run = 01
@password = Smoke-Test-Pass-1
@adminEmail = metro.admin.{{run}}@example.com
@customerEmail = metro.customer.{{run}}@example.com
@otpPurpose = REGISTER
# Paste the OTPs read from Redis here (step 1.1 / 1.5).
@adminOtp = 000000
@customerOtp = 000000

### 0. Gateway health (public)
GET {{baseUrl}}/actuator/health

### 1.1 Register (admin): send OTP
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/notifications/send-otp
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "email": "{{adminEmail}}",
  "purpose": "{{otpPurpose}}"
}

### 1.2 Register (admin): verify OTP -> expect "OTP verified" (status 200 in the body)
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/notifications/verify-otp?email={{adminEmail}}&otp={{adminOtp}}&purpose={{otpPurpose}}

### 1.3 Register (admin): create the user (must run within 5 minutes of 1.2)
# @name registerAdmin
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/users/register
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "email": "{{adminEmail}}",
  "name": "Metro Smoke Admin",
  "username": "metro_admin_{{run}}",
  "password": "{{password}}"
}

### 1.4 (outside this file) promote the admin to ROLE_ADMIN, see the Task 8 notes.

### 1.5 Register (customer): send OTP
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/notifications/send-otp
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "email": "{{customerEmail}}",
  "purpose": "{{otpPurpose}}"
}

### 1.6 Register (customer): verify OTP
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/notifications/verify-otp?email={{customerEmail}}&otp={{customerOtp}}&purpose={{otpPurpose}}

### 1.7 Register (customer): create the user
# @name registerCustomer
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/users/register
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "email": "{{customerEmail}}",
  "name": "Metro Smoke Customer",
  "username": "metro_customer_{{run}}",
  "password": "{{password}}"
}

### 2.1 Login (admin) -> Set-Cookie: token=<jwt with ROLE_ADMIN>
# @name loginAdmin
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/auth/local-login
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "usernameOrEmail": "{{adminEmail}}",
  "password": "{{password}}"
}

### 2.2 Who am I (admin) -> data.role = ROLE_ADMIN
GET {{baseUrl}}/api/users/me

### 3. Create station (ROLE_ADMIN). station-service publishes station.added; ticket-service logs
###    "Received station added event".
# @name createStation
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/stations
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "stationCode": "SMK{{run}}",
  "name": "Smoke Station {{run}}",
  "address": "1 Smoke Test Street, District 1, Ho Chi Minh City",
  "latitude": 10.7769,
  "longitude": 106.7009
}

### 3.1 Read it back (public)
GET {{baseUrl}}/api/stations/{{createStation.response.body.$.data.stationId}}

### 4. Create bus station (writes to MongoDB Atlas, database Bus, collection Bus)
# @name createBus
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/bus
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "name": "Smoke Bus Stop {{run}}",
  "code": "BUS-SMK-{{run}}",
  "address": "2 Smoke Test Street, District 1, Ho Chi Minh City",
  "latitude": 10.7772,
  "longitude": 106.7012,
  "isActive": 1
}

### 4.1 Read it back from Atlas (public)
GET {{baseUrl}}/api/bus/{{createBus.response.body.$.data.id}}

### 5. Create ticket type (ROLE_ADMIN)
# @name createTicketType
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/ts/ticket-types/create
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "name": "Smoke 1-day {{run}}",
  "description": "Created by http/aws-smoke.http",
  "price": 15000,
  "isActive": true,
  "validityDuration": 1
}

### 6.1 Login (customer) -> replaces the token cookie with a ROLE_CUSTOMER JWT
# @name loginCustomer
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/auth/local-login
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "usernameOrEmail": "{{customerEmail}}",
  "password": "{{password}}"
}

### 6.2 Payment method for the order (the orders DB starts empty)
# @name createPaymentMethod
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/orders/payment-methods/create-payment-method
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "paymentMethodName": "SMOKE-{{run}}"
}

### 6.3 Create order (ROLE_CUSTOMER): order -> ticket (Feign) -> user (Feign)
# @name createOrder
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/orders/create/days
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "ticketId": { "id": {{createTicketType.response.body.$.data.id}} },
  "paymentMethodId": {{createPaymentMethod.response.body.$.data.paymentMethodId}}
}

### 6.4 Mark the payment successful -> order-service publishes order.completed;
###     notification-service logs "Nhận event order.completed".
PUT {{baseUrl}}/api/orders/update-success/{{createOrder.response.body.$.data.orderId}}

### 6.5 Order is SUCCESSFUL
GET {{baseUrl}}/api/orders/{{createOrder.response.body.$.data.orderId}}
